## Why This Problem Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of dynamic programming on prefixes with a non-crossing pairing constraint. The core challenge is encoding the no-crossing condition by considering the last pair ending at position i with its left partner p.

## Key Concepts
- **DP on prefixes**: Extra dimension tracks the number of pairs formed
- **Non-crossing constraint**: Enforced by pairing i with p < i and using dp[p-1][t-1], which ensures all pairs lie completely to the left of p
- **Reconstruction**: Requires storing backpointers to output the actual pairs
- **Complexity**: Time O(n²k), Space O(nk)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPairs(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(NEG));
  const prev = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(null));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i - 1][t];
      prev[i][t] = { skip: true };

      for (let p = 1; p < i; p++) {
        const val = dp[p - 1][t - 1] + A[p - 1] * A[i - 1];
        if (val > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = val;
          prev[i][t] = { skip: false, p };
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const pairs = [];
  let i = n, t = k;
  while (i > 0 && t > 0) {
    if (prev[i][t].skip) {
      i--;
    } else {
      const p = prev[i][t].p;
      pairs.push([p, i]);
      i = p - 1;
      t--;
    }
  }

  return { value: dp[n][k], pairs: pairs.reverse() };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests 1D partition DP, median-based block costs, and reconstruction; mirrors realistic facility placement.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitions
- Median-based block cost
- Prefix sums for O(1) block cost
- Backpointer reconstruction
- Time/space trade-offs and potential optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
function minPostOffice(X, k) {
  const n = X.length;
  const cost = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(n).fill(0));

  // Precompute prefix sums for O(1) range sum queries
  const prefix = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + X[i];
  }

  // Precompute block costs using median property
  for (let l = 0; l < n; l++) {
    for (let r = l; r < n; r++) {
      const m = Math.floor((l + r) / 2);
      cost[l][r] = (X[m] * (m - l) - (prefix[m] - prefix[l])) +
                   ((prefix[r + 1] - prefix[m + 1]) - X[m] * (r - m));
    }
  }

  // DP table and backpointers
  const dp = Array.from({length: n + 1}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array.from({length: n + 1}, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let p = 1; p <= k; p++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = p - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const candidate = dp[j][p - 1] + cost[j][i - 1];
        if (candidate < dp[i][p]) {
          dp[i][p] = candidate;
          parent[i][p] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct solution
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n;
  for (let p = k; p >= 1; p--) {
    const start = parent[curr][p];
    blocks.unshift([start, curr - 1]);
    curr = start;
  }

  return { cost: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- Time: O(kn²) for DP transitions plus O(n²) for cost preprocessing
- Space: O(nk) for DP table, reducible to O(n) with layer optimization

## Why This Is Asked
Tests a new DP variant: maximizing the sum of non-additive subarray values (bitwise OR) while enforcing exactly k non-overlapping blocks. It also probes reconstruction logic for end-to-start blocks.

## Key Concepts
- DP over partitioning into k non-overlapping blocks
- Non-additive block value via OR, requiring on-the-fly accumulation
- Backpointers for exact block endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxKOrSubarrays(A, k){
  const n=A.length;
  const dp=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prv=Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let i=0;i<n;i++){
    for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
      let curr=0;
      for(let j=i;j>=0;j--){
        curr |= A[j];
        let left = dp[j][t-1];
        if(left>-Infinity){
          const cand = left + curr;
          if(cand>dp[i+1][t]){ dp[i+1][t]=cand; prv[i+1][t]=j; }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  return {value: dp[n][k], endAt: prv};
}
```

