## Why This Is Asked
This problem extends the classic Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LPS) problem by allowing up to k character deletions, requiring a 3D DP formulation. It tests understanding of subsequence DP with additional constraints, careful boundary handling, and the ability to reconstruct the solution path.

## Key Concepts
- Longest Palindromic Subsequence with deletions
- 3D DP (i, j, d) where d represents remaining deletions
- Path reconstruction for deletion decisions
- Time/space complexity: O(n²·k) time, O(n²·k) space

## Code Example
```javascript
function longestPalindromicSubseqWithDeletions(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n }, () =>
    Array.from({ length: n }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(0))
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
      dp[i][i][d] = 1;
    }
  }

  for (let len = 2; len <= n; len++) {
    for (let i = 0; i + len <= n; i++) {
      const j = i + len - 1;
      for (let d = 0; d <= k; d++) {
        if (s[i] === s[j]) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], (len > 2 ? dp[i + 1][j - 1][d] : 0) + 2);
        }
        if (d > 0) {
          dp[i][j][d] = Math.max(dp[i][j][d], dp[i + 1][j][d - 1], dp[i][j - 1][d - 1]);
        }
      }
    }
  }

  return dp[0][n - 1][k];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests understanding of partition dynamic programming with bitwise operations, commonly encountered in optimization scenarios for data processing and segmentation tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP formulation
- Subarray OR precomputation
- Boundary reconstruction
- Time/space complexity analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxOrPartition(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const OR = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(n+1).fill(0));

  // Precompute OR for all subarrays
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    let cur = 0;
    for (let j = i; j < n; j++) {
      cur |= A[j];
      OR[i][j+1] = cur;
    }
  }

  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const par = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
        if (dp[j][t-1] !== -Infinity) {
          const val = dp[j][t-1] + OR[j][i];
          if (val > dp[i][t]) {
            dp[i][t] = val;
            par[i][t] = j;
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct blocks
  const blocks = [];
  let curr = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    const start = par[curr][t];
    blocks.unshift([start, curr-1]);
    curr = start;
  }

  return { maxSum: dp[n][k], blocks };
}
```

## Complexity
Time: O(n²k) due to triple nested loops, Space: O(nk + n²) for DP tables and OR precomputation.

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests understanding of dynamic programming with non-overlapping segments where the segment value has a quadratic structure. It challenges candidates to handle state transitions, reconstruction, and complexity analysis systematically.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefix arrays with block count tracking
- Transition by considering all possible block endpoints
- Prefix sum optimization for O(1) subarray sum queries
- Backpointers for optimal solution reconstruction
- Time-space trade-off analysis

## Code Implementation
```javascript
function maxSubarrayValue(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const prefix = new Array(n + 1).fill(0);
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    prefix[i + 1] = prefix[i] + A[i];
  }

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prevStart = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      // Skip i
      if (dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]) {
        dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
        prevStart[i][t] = prevStart[i-1][t];
      }

      // End block at i
      for (let j = 1; j <= i; j++) {
        const blockValue = (prefix[i] - prefix[j-1]) * (i-j+1);
        const candidate = dp[j-1][t-1] + blockValue;
        if (candidate > dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = candidate;
          prevStart[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruction
  const result = [];
  let currI = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0 && currI > 0) {
    const start = prevStart[currI][currT];
    if (start !== -1 && start !== prevStart[currI-1][currT]) {
      result.unshift([start-1, currI-1]);
      currI = start - 1;
      currT--;
    } else {
      currI--;
    }
  }

  return { maxValue: dp[n][k], subarrays: result };
}
```

